
---
import '../styles/global.css';

const experiments = [
  {
    title: 'PnP Pose Estimation',
    description:
      'Feature-based camera pose estimation using image correspondences and RANSAC.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Image Pyramid',
    description:
      'Investigation of multi-scale feature detection and matching.',
  },
  {
    title: 'ICP Registration',
    description:
      'Estimation of relative motion through point-cloud alignment.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Synthetic Bunny',
    description:
      'Evaluation of geometric estimation in a controlled synthetic scene.',
  },
];
---

<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <meta
      name="description"
      content="A research and implementation project exploring visual SLAM, geometric motion estimation, PnP and ICP."
    />
    <title>Visual SLAM | IIIT Hyderabad</title>
  </head>

  <body>
    <main class="paper">
      <header class="paper-header">
        <h1>Visual SLAM: Camera Motion Estimation and Geometric Reconstruction</h1>

        <p class="authors">
          Anushka, Mehrish
        </p>

        <p class="affiliation">
          Robotics Research Centre, IIIT-H
        </p>

        <p class="paper-links">
          <a href="https://github.com/mehr1sh/slam" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">
            Code
          </a>
          <span>·</span>
          <a href="#method">Method</a>
          <span>·</span>
          <a href="#results">Results</a>
        </p>
      </header>

      <section>
        <h2>Abstract</h2>

        <p>
          Simultaneous Localisation and Mapping (SLAM) involves estimating
          the motion of a camera or robot while reconstructing its
          surrounding environment. This project focuses on implementing
          and evaluating geometric motion estimation techniques, with
          particular attention to feature-based pose estimation,
          point-cloud registration, and trajectory consistency.
        </p>

        <p>
          The implementation investigates Perspective-n-Point (PnP)
          estimation with RANSAC, multi-scale feature extraction, and
          Iterative Closest Point (ICP) registration. Experiments include
          controlled synthetic scenes and trajectory evaluation.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="method">
        <h2>Method</h2>

        <p>
          The system estimates camera motion from visual observations and
          geometric correspondences. The pipeline consists of feature
          extraction, feature matching, relative pose estimation, point-cloud
          alignment, and trajectory accumulation.
        </p>

        <h3>Feature extraction and matching</h3>
        <p>
          Image features are detected and matched across successive frames.
          Multi-scale image processing is investigated to improve feature
          detection and matching under changes in image scale.
        </p>

        <h3>Camera pose estimation</h3>
        <p>
          Perspective-n-Point (PnP) estimates the relative camera pose from
          2D–3D correspondences. RANSAC is used to reject outlier
          correspondences and obtain a robust pose estimate.
        </p>

        <h3>Point-cloud registration</h3>
        <p>
          Iterative Closest Point (ICP) estimates relative motion by aligning
          point clouds from successive observations. The estimated
          transformation is used to update the camera trajectory.
        </p>

        <h3>Trajectory estimation</h3>
        <p>
          Relative transformations are accumulated to obtain the camera
          trajectory. Consistent transformation conventions are necessary
          when composing successive relative poses.
        </p>

        <p class="equation">
          T<sub>wc,i+1</sub> =
          T<sub>wc,i</sub> · T<sub>i+1←i</sub><sup>−1</sup>
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="experiments">
        <h2>Experiments</h2>

        <p>
          The implementation is evaluated through a set of experiments
          investigating different components of the geometric estimation
          pipeline.
        </p>

        {experiments.map((experiment) => (
          <div class="experiment">
            <h3>{experiment.title}</h3>
            <p>{experiment.description}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </section>

      <section id="visualisation">
        <h2>Visualisation</h2>

        <p>
          A synthetic Bunny scene is used to inspect camera motion and
          geometric reconstruction. The visualisation is intended to show
          the estimated camera trajectory in relation to the reconstructed
          scene.
        </p>

        <figure>
          <img
            src="/slam/images/bunny-hero.png"
            alt="Synthetic Bunny scene used for SLAM visualisation"
          />
          <figcaption>
            Synthetic Bunny scene used for geometric estimation experiments.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </section>

      <section id="results">
        <h2>Results</h2>

        <p>
          The project evaluates estimated trajectories and geometric
          reconstruction against reference data. Quantitative comparisons
          and visual results will be presented here after the corresponding
          experiments have been verified and reproduced.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="reproducibility">
        <h2>Code and Reproducibility</h2>

        <p>
          The implementation and experiment code are available in the
          project repository.
        </p>

        <p>
          <a href="https://github.com/mehr1sh/slam" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">
            github.com/mehr1sh/slam
          </a>
        </p>
      </section>

      <footer>
        <p>
          Visual SLAM Research Project · IIIT Hyderabad
        </p>
      </footer>
    </main>
  </body>
</html>
